  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/5/51/Fantastic_Four_Vol_1_28.png",
  "plot_summary": "Reed and Sue are reading a newspaper article about the new heroes called the X-Men (and listing off the menaces they've faced - which curiously includes the \"Space Phantom\" - <i>uh</i>, did I <i>miss</i> something - I'm pretty sure it was the <i>Avengers</i> who tackled him).\n\nMeanwhile, we see the Puppet Master, who is responding to a message from the Mad Thinker, who's been reading up on the X-Men, trying to deduce what their leader, Professor X, looks like (hmm, again I'm confused - how does he know about the existence of Professor X - I'm more willing to let this slide on account of possibly learning about him from one of the X-Men already-defeated foes, but to my knowledge, they've left little record or memory of his existence - but okay, moving on...).\n\nThe Mad Thinker provides radioactive clay for the Puppet Master to use to fashion into Professor X, whose appearance the Thinker has calculated (<i>convenient</i>).&nbsp; Eventually, he succeeds, but Professor X, with his mental powers, is able to resist, and only with the Thinker's help with calculations is the Puppet Master finally able to gain control over him.\n\nProfessor X summons his X-Men and orders them to destroy the Fantastic Four!&nbsp; They can't believe it!&nbsp; But the professor claims to have tuned into their thoughts and determine \"that the Fantastic Four plan <i>world domination</i>!&nbsp; They must be stopped!\"\n\nThey visit the Baxter Building, pretending it's a casual visit, and Cyclops invites the FF to join them to investigate an alien ship sighting, but Reed is in the middle of an experiment.&nbsp; At this point, on page 6, Cyclops thinks \"<i>Good</i>!&nbsp; His answer is just what the Thinker <i>predicted</i> it would be!\"&nbsp; (This seems to be a major editorial blunder since the Thinker is only giving orders to the Puppet Master, and the X-Men aren't even <i>aware</i> at this point that he's even involved!)\n\nAt this point, the X-Men attack, and the battle is on!&nbsp; At the top of page 8, Ben says to Marvel Girl \"If you weren't a <i>female</i>, I'd <i>demolish</i> ya!&nbsp; But the only thing I can do to a nut like <i>you</i> is give ya the spankin' of your life!\"&nbsp; (<i>wow</i>)\n\nMr. Fantastic and the Beast's brief encounter comes with Mr. F's quip \"You're so in love with the sound of your own voice, that you forget that <i>others</i> can be dexterous too\" to which Beast (completely in character) responds \"I stand corrected, sir!\"&nbsp; (<i>well done</i>)\n\nReed finally orders the group to pretend to surrender so they can learn the motive behind the attack.&nbsp; Just to ensure they'll follow, the X-Men take Sue along with him, bound in ice.&nbsp; While they pursue, Ben speculates that if Reed and the Beast ever got into a conversation, that perhaps even Noah Webster wouldn't be able to keep up - to which Reed points out \"You don't fool us, Ben!&nbsp; <i>You</i> hold a college degree or two <i>yourself</i>!\"\n\nWhen they finally land, in a seemingly vacant area, Sue learns that the X-Men have no real idea what comes next - they're only obeying orders and have no idea why.&nbsp; The Professor's mental voice then commands them to battle the FF again \"and completely subdue them!\"&nbsp; He then leaves them to wonder <i>why</i>.\n\nWhen the FF arrive, they engage with the X-Men but soon run into booby traps, seemingly targeting them only!&nbsp; And they are subdued, allowing the Puppet Master and the Mad Thinker to finally reveal themselves and show the X-Men they've been duped.&nbsp; The Puppet Master then orders the professor to mentally cause them to fall asleep.&nbsp; They begin to succumb, but only the strong mind of the Beast is able to resist, and he attacks the Puppet Master and crushes the figure of the professor!\n\nReed has been watching and finally puzzles it all out, and when Ben finally frees him and the others, he orders the group to help the X-Men!&nbsp; Only the Mad Thinker's \"awesome android\" stands in the way of the combined groups' capture of the evil pair.&nbsp; But Professor X too is now freed, and he mentally helps them subdue the android.&nbsp; Naturally, the Mad Thinker has contingency plans, and the pair escape.\n\nThe X-Men apologize to the FF and then leave, with Ben admitting:&nbsp; \"They weren't too bad for amateurs!! 'cept for the <i>Beast</i> - he's too <i>ugly</i>!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "Write something",
  "subtitle": "Write something",
  "review_text": "This felt more like a contrived meeting between the groups, with the villains (not to mention the newspapers) having access to <i>way</i> more information than you'd expect.&nbsp; I give credit to how the Thinker was able to help the Puppet Master overcome the extra effort involved with taking over the Professor's will - that at least made sense, but how did he know about Professor X in the <i>first</i> place?\n\nFor the most part, this seemed more like a conversation between Stan and Jack that went something like this:&nbsp; \"Hey Jack, let's find some way to have the X-Men fight the FF.&nbsp; Maybe we can use the Puppet Master to take over the Professor's mind and order them to attack.\" and Jack coming back with \"but the Professor's mind is too strong!\"&nbsp; with the pair of them concluding that perhaps the Mad Thinker could assist, and then Jack goes home and draws out the book with Stan enjoying filling in the dialog (particularly between Reed and the Beast).&nbsp; It feels cheap somehow, like the fight between Giant-Man and Spider-Man this same month.\n\n<b>Major</b> editorial blunder with Cyclops somehow seeming to know that the Thinker was involved on page 6.&nbsp; He also calls Marvel Girl \"Jean\" in the middle of the battle later on (showing that even Stan Lee could use some editorial help from time to time).&nbsp; And the Thing's VMA of the month, telling Jean that she's about to get the \"spankin' of your life!\" (<i>geez</i>)\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nAs nice as it was to see the two groups encounter each other, the story was subpar, and it all felt too contrived.&nbsp; Another of the comic book tropes strikes again - the heroes engage in battle before they discover it's a misunderstanding and they join forces to take out a shared menace.&nbsp; Not good enough for even a mid-grade rating here.\n\nSide Notes:\nIn this issue, we learn that Ben holds at least one college degree - perhaps even two.&nbsp; This seems surprising, but there it is, at the top of page 13!\n\nAnd how's about a tip of the hat to letterer Art Simek, who I <i>suspect</i> might be responsible for the amusing credit box comments, often saving the best for himself as he does in this issue!&nbsp; <i>Ha</i>!&nbsp; There's even a letter in this issue commending the team on the clever \"adjectives\" such as \"Darling Stan Lee\" and \"Dazzling Dick Ayers\" - or the remarks in (parentheses).&nbsp; And it's signs off with:&nbsp; Sloppily typed by...Dave (When will he ever be satisfied?) Bibby!&nbsp; (<i>Nice</i>!)\n\nIn the <i>Announcements</i> section, the whole \"Bruce\" vs \"Bob\" Banner thing is made clear.&nbsp; Admitting that they goofed in a couple of different issues, they state:&nbsp; <i>Well, there's only one thing to do - we're gonna take the cowardly way out.&nbsp; From now on his name is ROBERT BRUCE BANNER - so we can't go wrong no matter WHAT we call him! (Unless it comes out \"Seymour\"!)</i>\n(and to cap it off, the <i>Announcements</i> in <i>Avengers </i>#6 hints that in issue 7, the Hulk might appear:&nbsp; <i>And THIS time we'll try to remember what his first name is!</i>",
  "highlight": "Write something"
}